  "worked_solution": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\frac{1+y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\u222c}_{R}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A+{\u222c}_{R}y\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A & =(8-0){\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}{|}_{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}-(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}y\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & =(2-1){\\int }_{0}^{8}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{8}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\frac{1+y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}A=3+32=35.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
